    ideal: 'kill sends SIGTERM by default, which asks the process to terminate; the process can catch it to finish requests, flush data and exit gracefully. kill 9 sends SIGKILL, which cannot be caught or ignored: the kernel stops the process immediately, so there is no cleanup and you risk corrupted files or lost data. It should be the last resort. A zombie process is a child that has exited but whose parent has not yet called wait to reap its exit status, so it remains in the process table with state Z in ps. It uses no CPU or memory, and killing the zombie does nothing; you fix the parent or kill the parent so init adopts and reaps it. For services I manage processes with systemctl and systemd, which sends SIGTERM then SIGKILL after a timeout.',
